module gunbc.runner.runner_host_image_store

import std.types { Bool, Int, List, NonEmptyStr, String }

// WHERE HOST IMAGES LIVE, HOW LONG THEY LIVE, AND WHAT DECIDES.
//
// NINETEEN IMAGES ACCUMULATED ON ONE NFS EXPORT DURING ONE SESSION OF QUALIFICATION WORK, 6.6 GB,
// with names an author invented as he went -- slice6b, slice7c, runner4, signed2. Nothing deleted
// them because nothing owned them: each was published by hand at the moment it was needed, and the
// question of when it stopped being needed had no answer anywhere.

// ── identity ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// AN IMAGE IS ITS DIGEST. The filenames above are NICKNAMES for content, and they have every defect
// a nickname has: "runner4" tells a reader nothing about what is in the file, two builds could be
// given one name, and one build published twice gets two. The receipts already got this right --
// they cite ISO digests -- which is the only reason the retention rule below is decidable at all.
//
// THE NAME IS A CONVENIENCE FOR THE BMC, WHICH ADDRESSES MEDIA BY FILENAME. That is an external
// constraint and it is fine; what is not fine is letting the filename be the identity anywhere else.
// A store that names files by digest and keeps the human label as an attribute has one authority
// for what an image IS and a separate, disposable one for what to call it.
type ImageIdentity
  = ImageByDigest { sha256: NonEmptyStr }
  | ImageByAuthorChosenName { name: NonEmptyStr, defect: NonEmptyStr }

data image_naming_defect: ImageIdentity = ImageByAuthorChosenName {
  name: "gunbc-host-slice6b.iso, gunbc-host-runner4.iso, gunbc-host-signed2.iso and sixteen others",
  defect: "the name is a nickname for content: it does not say what the image contains, it can be reused across builds, and it multiplies when one build is published twice",
}

// ── retention: what makes an image worth keeping ───────────────────────────────────────────────

// TWO REASONS TO KEEP AN IMAGE AND NO OTHERS. Either a committed receipt cites its digest -- in which
// case deleting it would make a published claim uncheckable -- or the BMC is currently redirecting
// it, in which case deleting it pulls the medium out from under a host that may be booting.
//
// EVERYTHING ELSE IS GARBAGE BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by an author's judgement about whether he
// might want it later. "I might need this" is what produced nineteen images.
type ImageRetention
  = RetainedCitedByReceipt { receipt: NonEmptyStr }
  | RetainedAttachedAtBmc
  | Unreferenced

data retention_rule: NonEmptyStr = "an image is retained if and only if a committed receipt cites its digest, or the BMC is currently redirecting it; the reap deletes the rest"

// THE ATTACHMENT STATE IS READ FROM THE REDIRECTION CONFIGURATION, AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THE
// REAP READ THE WRONG FIELD. It asked media/general for cd_image_name and got an empty string while
// the BMC was actively redirecting an image; the authority is remote/configurations, whose rows
// carry image_name alongside a redirection_status.
//
// THAT MISTAKE WAS HARMLESS ONLY BY LUCK -- the attached image happened to also be cited by a
// receipt, so the wrong answer and the right answer agreed. A check that is wrong and looks right is
// the failure this lane keeps finding, and it found it again inside its own cleanup.
data attachment_authority: NonEmptyStr = "remote/configurations image_name, not media/general cd_image_name; the latter read empty while an image was redirected"

// AND AN UNREADABLE ATTACHMENT STATE REFUSES RATHER THAN DEFAULTING TO "nothing is attached".
// "I could not ask the BMC" and "the BMC says nothing is attached" are different facts, and only one
// of them makes a deletion safe. Treating them alike is the absorbing arm: the reap would proceed
// on ignorance in the direction that destroys data.
data unreadable_attachment_arm: NonEmptyStr = "if the BMC's redirection state cannot be read the reap deletes nothing and says so; absence of an answer is not an answer"

// ── what the first reap did ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

data first_reap: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  "20 host images examined, 6.9 GB",
  "3 retained: their digests are cited by runner_two_attempt_receipt, runner_bound_attempt_receipt and runner_signed_envelope_receipt",
  "1 of those 3 was also the image the BMC was redirecting",
  "17 deleted, 5.8 GB reclaimed",
  "images this lane did not create -- an Alpine ISO, memtest, an Ubuntu installer -- were not considered and not touched",
]

// ── the store itself, and the part of the ask that is NOT yet answered ─────────────────────────

// THE EXPORT IS KNOWN AND IT IS NOT RESILIENT, and those are separate properties that a single
// phrase would blur. KNOWN means the BMC's redirection configuration names the serving host and the
// path, so anyone who asks it can find an image -- it does NOT mean this module knows the answer,
// and an earlier revision spelled a host here and was wrong about it for weeks. RESILIENT is the
// separate question: it is one machine's local filesystem with no replication and no backup, so a
// disk failure loses every image a receipt cites -- and with them the ability to check any claim
// those receipts make about what booted.
//
// THE RECEIPTS SURVIVE THAT LOSS AND THE VERIFIABILITY DOES NOT. A digest in a committed .dag file
// is durable; the bytes it identifies are not. That asymmetry is the actual gap, and calling the
// export "known/resilient" as one property would hide it.
// THE LOCATION FIELD IS GONE, AND ITS DELETION IS THE CENSUS.
//
// It carried "/srv/bmc on srv1" as an ordinary string. That was FALSE: there is no /srv/bmc on
// srv1. The export is on srv2, read-only to the Mt. Collins controller, and the images were intact
// there the whole time -- measured 2026-09-10, after an operator reported the srv1 path empty and a
// downstream analysis had already repeated the claim as a corpus fact.
//
// THE DEFECT IS NOT THE WRONG HOSTNAME, IT IS THAT A HOSTNAME WAS SPELLED HERE AT ALL. This
// module's own attachment_authority names the BMC's redirection configuration as the authority for
// which image is presented, and that same configuration is what names WHERE it is read from. A
// literal beside it is a second answer to a question this module already routes elsewhere (DESIGN
// §3), and a positional one at that: nothing edits this string when an export moves, so it decays
// silently while every reader keeps trusting it.
//
// CORRECTING THE STRING WAS THE TEMPTING MOVE AND IT IS THE WRONG ONE -- a freshly-true literal
// rots exactly as the last one did, and restores the false confidence that made this defect
// expensive. So the field is deleted rather than fixed (§3 delete-first: uproot the root, then fix
// forward), and what remains are the two properties that are genuinely facts ABOUT THE STORE
// rather than about where it happens to sit.
type StoreStanding {
  known: Bool
  resilient: Bool
  what_loss_costs: NonEmptyStr
}

data host_image_store: StoreStanding = StoreStanding {
  known: true,
  resilient: false,
  what_loss_costs: "every receipt that cites an ISO digest becomes uncheckable: the claim survives in git and the bytes it names do not",
}

// WHAT IT TAKES TO ANSWER "WHERE" AGAIN, stated as the capability and not as an artifact that would
// contribute to one: a modeled read of the BMC's remote/configurations that yields the serving host
// and export path as an OBSERVATION with its read time, so the location is derived at the moment it
// is asked rather than asserted once and trusted forever. Nothing short of that restores the field.
// Until then this module answers WHAT the store guarantees and refuses to answer WHERE, which is
// the honest pair: it never knew where, it only recorded where someone believed it was.

// ── the deeper defect the reap does not fix ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THE IMAGES WERE PUBLISHED BY HAND, one at a time, by an author running a copy command. The reap
// gives that a garbage collector; it does not give it a producer. A store whose writes are
// out-of-band and whose deletes are modeled is still half unmanaged, and the half that is unmanaged
// is the one that decides what exists.
//
// THE TERMINAL FORM IS THE BUILD PUBLISHING ITS OWN OUTPUT under a digest-derived name, with the
// human label carried as an attribute and the retention rule evaluated on every publish rather than
// when someone remembers. That is the same shape the rest of this lane keeps arriving at: the
// hand-built image tree under /var/tmp is parallel authority to gunbc.runner_guest_image, and this
// store is parallel authority to whatever will eventually own artifact publication.
data publication_standing: NonEmptyStr = "publication is still an out-of-band copy performed by an author; the reap manages deletion only, and a store with modeled deletes and unmodeled writes is half unmanaged"

data next_steps: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  "name stored images by digest and carry the human label as an attribute, so the BMC's filename requirement stops being the identity",
  "run the retention rule on every publish rather than when someone notices, so an unreferenced image never accumulates in the first place",
  "decide what resilience the store owes: replication, or an accepted standing that receipts older than the store's retention are uncheckable and say so",
  "drive publication from the model, which also removes the hand-built image tree that produced these names",
]
